<sn><md>### Simpler Linux option: store credentials in a file

You can tell Git to remember credentials:

```bash
git config --global credential.helper store
```

Then:

```bash
git push
```

Enter:

```text
Username: your-github-username
Password: your-github-token
```

Git saves them in:

```bash
~/.git-credentials
```

You can check:

```bash
cat ~/.git-credentials
```

It will look approximately like:

```text
https://USERNAME:TOKEN@github.com
```

&gt; **This is not encrypted**, so [I don't recommend this on a shared or insecure computer]{.fg-red}.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Better option for your Ubuntu system: SSH

For long-term GitHub use, I would actually recommend **SSH authentication** instead of storing a GitHub token.

You create an SSH key:

```bash
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "your-email@example.com"
```

Then add the public key:

```bash
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
```

to GitHub.

After that, your repository URL can be:

```bash
git@github.com:USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git
```

and:

```bash
git pull
git push
```

will work without entering your GitHub username or token.
</md></sn><sn><md>### In short

For your Ubuntu setup:

```text
HTTPS + credential manager
        ↓
Username + token stored securely
        ↓
git pull / git push
```

or, preferably:

```text
SSH key
   ↓
GitHub
   ↓
git pull / git push
```

**Don't put your GitHub token directly into scripts, `.gitconfig`, or repository files.** Also, if you've already exposed a token publicly, revoke it and create a new one.
</md></sn>